WEBVTT

NOTE week09-clip1-rag
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.822 line:2 align:center
A language model learns from public text,

2
00:00:02.822 --> 00:00:06.702 line:2 align:center
so it cannot know your
hospital's own protocols.

3
00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:12.307 line:2 align:center
Asked when antibiotics must start under
our sepsis protocol, it says three hours:

4
00:00:12.307 --> 00:00:16.817 line:2 align:center
a reasonable national
figure, but not our rule.

5
00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:21.550 line:2 align:center
Retrieval starts by splitting the
hospital's documents into chunks.

6
00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:27.744 line:2 align:center
Almanac used chunks of 1,000 tokens. Each
chunk is embedded once into a vector,

7
00:00:27.744 --> 00:00:32.683 line:2 align:center
which in Almanac had 1,536 numbers.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:37.742 line:2 align:center
At question time, the question
is embedded the same way

9
00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:41.187 line:2 align:center
and compared with every
chunk by cosine similarity.

10
00:00:41.187 --> 00:00:50.683 line:2 align:center
Here the 3 best chunks
score 0.86, 0.79, and 0.62.

11
00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:56.792 line:2 align:center
Only chunks that score above a threshold
are passed on. Almanac used a threshold,

12
00:00:56.792 --> 00:01:05.217 line:2 align:center
lambda, of 0.83, so here only the first
chunk, from the sepsis protocol, passes.

13
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:10.068 line:2 align:center
A second question, about rattlesnake
bites, finds no good match:

14
00:01:10.068 --> 00:01:13.123 line:2 align:center
its best score is only 0.71.

15
00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:18.649 line:2 align:center
The published Almanac then falls
back on the model's own knowledge.

16
00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:20.972 line:2 align:center
The preprint version abstained instead,

17
00:01:20.972 --> 00:01:25.217 line:2 align:center
and said the answer was
not found in the sources.

18
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:29.736 line:2 align:center
The passing chunk is pasted into the
prompt, together with its source.

19
00:01:29.736 --> 00:01:35.772 line:2 align:center
The model now answers within one hour,
which is what the synthetic protocol says,

20
00:01:35.772 --> 00:01:39.350 line:2 align:center
and it cites section two of that protocol.

21
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.892 line:2 align:center
Clinicians compared
Almanac with ChatGPT-4,

22
00:01:42.892 --> 00:01:50.899 line:2 align:center
Bing, and Bard on 314 clinical questions.
Almanac ranked first for factuality,

23
00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:58.683 line:2 align:center
completeness, and preference, and its
citations were correct 91% of the time.

24
00:01:58.883 --> 00:02:02.784 line:2 align:center
Read these results with care.
Against adversarial prompts,

25
00:02:02.784 --> 00:02:08.325 line:2 align:center
Almanac scored 100% and ChatGPT-4 7%,

26
00:02:08.325 --> 00:02:10.680 line:2 align:center
mostly because Almanac declined to answer.

27
00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.861 line:2 align:center
The questions and the grading came
from the study's own clinicians,

28
00:02:15.861 --> 00:02:19.513 line:2 align:center
and only 25 questions are public.
